window.ICSE_PRACTICE=window.ICSE_PRACTICE||{};window.ICSE_PRACTICE["histciv"]={"bank":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.","answer":"It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-007","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain four safeguards of judicial independence.","answer":"Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings. Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-018","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings.","Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain judicial review and name three kinds of government action that may raise a constitutional challenge.","answer":"Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution. A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed. Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-020","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution.","A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed.","Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four changes in British policy or administration after the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-028","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Compare Early and Assertive Nationalists using aims, methods, attitude to British rule and mass participation.","answer":"Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion; Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation. Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-034","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion;","Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation.","Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"State three early objectives of the Indian National Congress.","answer":"Promote contacts and unity among political workers; develop national feeling beyond regional/religious divisions; formulate and present common demands and train public opinion.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-035","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Promote contacts and unity among political workers; develop national feeling beyond regional/religious divisions; formulate and present common demands and train public opinion."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain four ways the Partition of Bengal stimulated the national movement.","answer":"It was opposed as division of a politically active region; boycott of foreign goods expanded; Swadeshi industries/education received support; new public participation and assertive methods developed. Explain events and responses rather than merely listing dates.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-036","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It was opposed as division of a politically active region; boycott of foreign goods expanded;","Swadeshi industries/education received support; new public participation and assertive methods developed.","Explain events and responses rather than merely listing dates."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain four factors contributing to the formation of the Muslim League.","answer":"British encouragement of separate political identities; elite concerns about representation; the Simla Deputation/separate-electorate demand; perceived need to protect Muslim political interests in the contemporary setting. Avoid presenting the community as having one uniform view.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-038","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["British encouragement of separate political identities; elite concerns about representation; the Simla Deputation/separate-electorate demand; perceived need to protect Muslim political interests in the contemporary setting.","Avoid presenting the community as having one uniform view."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain four features of the Non-Cooperation programme.","answer":"Surrender of titles; boycott of government educational institutions/courts/elections as proposed; boycott of foreign cloth; constructive promotion of khadi, national education and Hindu-Muslim unity. Specify that this was intended as non-violent withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-042","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Surrender of titles; boycott of government educational institutions/courts/elections as proposed; boycott of foreign cloth; constructive promotion of khadi, national education and Hindu-Muslim unity.","Specify that this was intended as non-violent withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.","answer":"Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-043","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline;","Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"State four clauses of the Gandhi-Irwin Pact, including at least one Congress undertaking.","answer":"Government undertook release of political prisoners not convicted of violence, withdrawal of ordinances/prosecutions, restoration of eligible confiscated property and permission for peaceful salt collection/picketing. Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and attend the Second Round Table Conference. Any four accurate clauses, with both sides represented.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-044","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Government undertook release of political prisoners not convicted of violence, withdrawal of ordinances/prosecutions, restoration of eligible confiscated property and permission for peaceful salt collection/picketing.","Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and attend the Second Round Table Conference.","Any four accurate clauses, with both sides represented."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.","answer":"Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-047","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances;","Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930.","Both were intended to be non-violent."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain four effects of the Gandhian mass movements on Indian politics.","answer":"Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule. Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-048","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule.","Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet Mission’s aim of preserving unity from the final 1947 partition arrangement using four points.","answer":"Cabinet Mission proposed a union with limited central subjects and provincial grouping; it sought to avoid division into sovereign states. The 1947 plan accepted partition and two successor dominions; transfer of power followed with different constitutional arrangements. Explain differences rather than mixing clauses from the two plans.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-054","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Cabinet Mission proposed a union with limited central subjects and provincial grouping; it sought to avoid division into sovereign states.","The 1947 plan accepted partition and two successor dominions; transfer of power followed with different constitutional arrangements.","Explain differences rather than mixing clauses from the two plans."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four long-term causes of the First World War.","answer":"Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems. Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-056","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems.","Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four factors helping the rise of dictatorships in inter-war Europe.","answer":"Economic distress/unemployment; resentment over peace settlements or disappointed national aims; weak parliamentary governments; fear of communism and propaganda/paramilitary methods. Relate factors accurately to Germany/Italy as appropriate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-058","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Economic distress/unemployment; resentment over peace settlements or disappointed national aims; weak parliamentary governments; fear of communism and propaganda/paramilitary methods.","Relate factors accurately to Germany/Italy as appropriate."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four causes of the Second World War.","answer":"Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland. Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-060","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland.","Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Describe the composition and voting of the Security Council using four points.","answer":"Fifteen members; five permanent members; ten elected non-permanent members with two-year terms; substantive decisions require the prescribed majority without a negative vote/veto by a permanent member. Do not say every member has veto power.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-065","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen members; five permanent members; ten elected non-permanent members with two-year terms; substantive decisions require the prescribed majority without a negative vote/veto by a permanent member.","Do not say every member has veto power."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four distinctions between the General Assembly and Security Council.","answer":"Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-067","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.","answer":"Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-071","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation.","Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain why the UN and NAM are not interchangeable organisations using four distinctions.","answer":"UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation; NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy. Their membership principles differ; UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ; NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-073","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation;","NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy.","Their membership principles differ;","UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ;","NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare the role of armed rebellion in 1857 with the intended discipline of Gandhian mass movements. Use methods, leadership and participation.","answer":"1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives; Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation. Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-074","section":"Comparison and application","family":"1857 / Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives;","Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation.","Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.","answer":"President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-076","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Parliament / Executive","solutionSteps":["President is constitutional head;","Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy;","Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?","answer":"Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-077","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Judiciary / Executive","solutionSteps":["Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity.","Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other."]}],"papers":[{"id":"histciv-paper-01","number":1,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 01","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Rowlatt Act","greased cartridges","the Vernacular Press Act","the Salt Law"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["President’s electoral college","Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["First World War","formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe","Russian Revolution"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies","membership of political parties only","merits of every parliamentary speech"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["ILO","UNESCO","WHO","FAO"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["four years","five years","six years","permanent with retiring thirds"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1919","1905","1885","1857"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","State High Court"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Ripon","Wavell","Mountbatten","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Cold War","First World War","Second World War","Korean War"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1942","1885","1916"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["independent judgement outside military power blocs","refusal to join the UN","automatic support for one bloc","isolation from all world affairs"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["Indian National Congress","French government","British Crown","United Nations"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1947","1942","1930","1920"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March"],"correct":0}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is a bicameral legislature? Name the two Houses of India’s Parliament.","answer":"A legislature with two chambers; Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the principal leader associated with the INA’s later campaign and give one objective.","answer":"Subhas Chandra Bose; armed struggle to liberate India from British rule.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn in 1922? Name the incident.","answer":"Chauri Chaura violence, including burning of a police station; Gandhi judged the movement insufficiently disciplined in non-violence.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain why the UN and NAM are not interchangeable organisations using four distinctions.","answer":"UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation; NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy. Their membership principles differ; UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ; NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-073","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation;","NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy.","Their membership principles differ;","UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ;","NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-02","number":2,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 02","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","five years","four years","six years"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1930","1947","1920","1942"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Chauri Chaura","Dandi March","Mountbatten Plan"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["UNESCO","FAO","WHO","ILO"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Supreme Court","President’s electoral college","Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1930","1919","1942","1922"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Rajya Sabha","State High Court","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["First World War","Korean War","Cold War","Second World War"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["independent judgement outside military power blocs","automatic support for one bloc","refusal to join the UN","isolation from all world affairs"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Mountbatten","Ripon","Wavell","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UN Security Council","UNESCO executive office","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["French government","Indian National Congress","United Nations","British Crown"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Vernacular Press Act","the Rowlatt Act","the Salt Law","greased cartridges"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["continued Company rule","a permanent single British province","two independent dominions","a new League of Nations"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["defeat in an election","loss in a civil contract","unlawful detention","unpaid income tax"],"correct":2}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Distinguish non-alignment from isolation.","answer":"Non-alignment means independent judgement outside rival military blocs; isolation means withdrawal from international engagement. NAM favours active engagement rather than isolation.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What law did the Dandi March challenge, and in which year?","answer":"The British salt monopoly/tax law; 1930.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is the purpose of habeas corpus? What can the court require?","answer":"Protection against unlawful detention; production of the detained person and justification of detention, with release if unlawful.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?","answer":"Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-077","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Judiciary / Executive","solutionSteps":["Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity.","Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.","answer":"Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-047","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances;","Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930.","Both were intended to be non-violent."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four causes of the Second World War.","answer":"Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland. Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-060","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland.","Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.","answer":"Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-071","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation.","Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-03","number":3,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 03","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Japan","Italy alone","the United States","Germany"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","four years","five years","six years"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["Russian Revolution","formation of the Congress","First World War","Second World War in Europe"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","a permanent single British province","continued Company rule","a new League of Nations"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["WHO","FAO","UNESCO","ILO"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","President’s electoral college","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","President","Speaker"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["British Crown","Indian National Congress","United Nations","French government"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unlawful detention","defeat in an election","loss in a civil contract","unpaid income tax"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1916","1942","1885"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Dalhousie","Ripon","Wavell","Mountbatten"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1919","1885","1857","1905"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1920","1947","1942","1930"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["isolation from all world affairs","independent judgement outside military power blocs","automatic support for one bloc","refusal to join the UN"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["State High Court","Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":2}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"What was the Doctrine of Lapse? Name the Governor-General associated with it.","answer":"Annexation of certain states when a ruler died without a recognised natural heir under the policy; Dalhousie.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the 1939 invasion that triggered war in Europe and two countries that then declared war on Germany.","answer":"Invasion of Poland; Britain and France.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is a bicameral legislature? Name the two Houses of India’s Parliament.","answer":"A legislature with two chambers; Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four changes in British policy or administration after the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-028","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-04","number":4,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 04","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["membership of political parties only","profitability of companies","merits of every parliamentary speech","constitutional validity of law or executive action"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","the Vernacular Press Act","the Rowlatt Act","greased cartridges"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["ILO","WHO","FAO","UNESCO"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Rajya Sabha","Supreme Court","State High Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1930","1919","1922","1942"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["continued Company rule","a new League of Nations","a permanent single British province","two independent dominions"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Dandi March"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["First World War","Second World War","Korean War","Cold War"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Dalhousie","Wavell","Ripon","Mountbatten"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["Second World War in Europe","First World War","Russian Revolution","formation of the Congress"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Ripon","Curzon","Mountbatten","Dalhousie"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["United Nations","Indian National Congress","French government","British Crown"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unpaid income tax","loss in a civil contract","unlawful detention","defeat in an election"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1916","1906","1942","1885"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","Japan","the United States","Germany"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Lok Sabha Speaker","Prime Minister","Vice-President","President"],"correct":2}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"What is a bicameral legislature? Name the two Houses of India’s Parliament.","answer":"A legislature with two chambers; Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name two Assertive Nationalist leaders.","answer":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lala Lajpat Rai: any two.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate trigger of World War I and the treaty imposed on Germany after it.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Treaty of Versailles.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the principal leader associated with the INA’s later campaign and give one objective.","answer":"Subhas Chandra Bose; armed struggle to liberate India from British rule.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the pact of 1916 between Congress and the Muslim League and state one significance.","answer":"Lucknow Pact; joint constitutional demands/cooperation between the organisations, including an agreed representation scheme.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish non-alignment from isolation.","answer":"Non-alignment means independent judgement outside rival military blocs; isolation means withdrawal from international engagement. NAM favours active engagement rather than isolation.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.","answer":"It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-007","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?","answer":"Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-077","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Judiciary / Executive","solutionSteps":["Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity.","Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.","answer":"Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-043","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline;","Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain four features of the Non-Cooperation programme.","answer":"Surrender of titles; boycott of government educational institutions/courts/elections as proposed; boycott of foreign cloth; constructive promotion of khadi, national education and Hindu-Muslim unity. Specify that this was intended as non-violent withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-042","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Surrender of titles; boycott of government educational institutions/courts/elections as proposed; boycott of foreign cloth; constructive promotion of khadi, national education and Hindu-Muslim unity.","Specify that this was intended as non-violent withdrawal of cooperation."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four distinctions between the General Assembly and Security Council.","answer":"Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-067","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-05","number":5,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 05","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["refusal to join the UN","independent judgement outside military power blocs","automatic support for one bloc","isolation from all world affairs"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["First World War","Korean War","Second World War","Cold War"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","four years","six years","five years"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Chauri Chaura","Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Election Commission","Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Vernacular Press Act","greased cartridges","the Salt Law","the Rowlatt Act"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Dalhousie","Curzon","Ripon","Mountbatten"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a permanent single British province","two independent dominions","a new League of Nations","continued Company rule"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1942","1885","1916","1906"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Cabinet Secretary","President","Chief Justice"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unpaid income tax","defeat in an election","unlawful detention","loss in a civil contract"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["French government","British Crown","United Nations","Indian National Congress"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["the United States","Italy alone","Germany","Japan"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Wavell","Dalhousie","Ripon","Mountbatten"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish non-alignment from isolation.","answer":"Non-alignment means independent judgement outside rival military blocs; isolation means withdrawal from international engagement. NAM favours active engagement rather than isolation.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was the Doctrine of Lapse? Name the Governor-General associated with it.","answer":"Annexation of certain states when a ruler died without a recognised natural heir under the policy; Dalhousie.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is the purpose of habeas corpus? What can the court require?","answer":"Protection against unlawful detention; production of the detained person and justification of detention, with release if unlawful.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the 1939 invasion that triggered war in Europe and two countries that then declared war on Germany.","answer":"Invasion of Poland; Britain and France.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn in 1922? Name the incident.","answer":"Chauri Chaura violence, including burning of a police station; Gandhi judged the movement insufficiently disciplined in non-violence.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"State three early objectives of the Indian National Congress.","answer":"Promote contacts and unity among political workers; develop national feeling beyond regional/religious divisions; formulate and present common demands and train public opinion.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-035","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Promote contacts and unity among political workers; develop national feeling beyond regional/religious divisions; formulate and present common demands and train public opinion."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain four ways the Partition of Bengal stimulated the national movement.","answer":"It was opposed as division of a politically active region; boycott of foreign goods expanded; Swadeshi industries/education received support; new public participation and assertive methods developed. Explain events and responses rather than merely listing dates.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-036","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It was opposed as division of a politically active region; boycott of foreign goods expanded;","Swadeshi industries/education received support; new public participation and assertive methods developed.","Explain events and responses rather than merely listing dates."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four causes of the Second World War.","answer":"Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland. Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-060","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland.","Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Describe the composition and voting of the Security Council using four points.","answer":"Fifteen members; five permanent members; ten elected non-permanent members with two-year terms; substantive decisions require the prescribed majority without a negative vote/veto by a permanent member. Do not say every member has veto power.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-065","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen members; five permanent members; ten elected non-permanent members with two-year terms; substantive decisions require the prescribed majority without a negative vote/veto by a permanent member.","Do not say every member has veto power."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-06","number":6,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 06","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act","the Vernacular Press Act"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1942","1922","1919","1930"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Dandi March","Chauri Chaura","Mountbatten Plan","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha alone","President’s electoral college","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1947","1942","1920","1930"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly","UN Security Council","UNESCO executive office"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["four years","six years","five years","permanent with retiring thirds"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Wavell","Ripon","Mountbatten","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Election Commission","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["President","Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","Speaker"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1942","1906","1916","1885"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","a new League of Nations","a permanent single British province","continued Company rule"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unlawful detention","defeat in an election","unpaid income tax","loss in a civil contract"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["First World War","Russian Revolution","formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","membership of political parties only","constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies"],"correct":2}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the chief fields of work of WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: health; UNESCO: education, science and culture.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two major administrative consequences of the 1857 uprising.","answer":"Company rule ended and Crown rule began; office of Secretary of State for India created. Accept appropriate army reorganisation or changed princely-state policy as distinct consequences.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers? To which House is it owed?","answer":"The ministry stands or falls together as a body and must retain the confidence of the Lok Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the 1939 invasion that triggered war in Europe and two countries that then declared war on Germany.","answer":"Invasion of Poland; Britain and France.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four changes in British policy or administration after the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-028","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.","answer":"Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-047","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances;","Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930.","Both were intended to be non-violent."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.","answer":"Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-071","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation.","Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-07","number":7,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 07","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","a permanent single British province","a new League of Nations","continued Company rule"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Germany","Italy alone","Japan","the United States"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Korean War","Cold War","Second World War","First World War"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","Election Commission"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["formation of the Congress","First World War","Second World War in Europe","Russian Revolution"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly","UNESCO executive office"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1930","1919","1922","1942"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1885","1857","1905","1919"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1920","1942","1930","1947"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","constitutional validity of law or executive action","membership of political parties only","profitability of companies"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["automatic support for one bloc","refusal to join the UN","isolation from all world affairs","independent judgement outside military power blocs"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["French government","United Nations","British Crown","Indian National Congress"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["UNESCO","WHO","ILO","FAO"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unlawful detention","unpaid income tax","loss in a civil contract","defeat in an election"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Dalhousie","Ripon","Mountbatten","Wavell"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha alone","President’s electoral college","Supreme Court"],"correct":0}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"What is a bicameral legislature? Name the two Houses of India’s Parliament.","answer":"A legislature with two chambers; Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the normal term of the Lok Sabha and the term of a Rajya Sabha member.","answer":"Five years; six years respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain judicial review and name three kinds of government action that may raise a constitutional challenge.","answer":"Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution. A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed. Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-020","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution.","A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed.","Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare the role of armed rebellion in 1857 with the intended discipline of Gandhian mass movements. Use methods, leadership and participation.","answer":"1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives; Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation. Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-074","section":"Comparison and application","family":"1857 / Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives;","Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation.","Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four long-term causes of the First World War.","answer":"Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems. Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-056","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems.","Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain why the UN and NAM are not interchangeable organisations using four distinctions.","answer":"UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation; NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy. Their membership principles differ; UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ; NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-073","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation;","NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy.","Their membership principles differ;","UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ;","NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-08","number":8,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 08","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Chauri Chaura","Mountbatten Plan","Cabinet Mission","Dandi March"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1885","1916","1942"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["six years","four years","permanent with retiring thirds","five years"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["independent judgement outside military power blocs","automatic support for one bloc","refusal to join the UN","isolation from all world affairs"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["United Nations","French government","Indian National Congress","British Crown"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1942","1919","1930","1922"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker","President","Vice-President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","the Rowlatt Act","greased cartridges","the Vernacular Press Act"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Cold War","First World War","Korean War","Second World War"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["State High Court","Rajya Sabha","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["WHO","ILO","FAO","UNESCO"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Ripon","Wavell","Dalhousie","Mountbatten"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Election Commission","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha alone"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies","membership of political parties only","merits of every parliamentary speech"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","World Health Assembly","NAM secretariat","UN Security Council"],"correct":3}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the normal term of the Lok Sabha and the term of a Rajya Sabha member.","answer":"Five years; six years respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two principal aims of the UN.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations/cooperation and promote human rights. Award two distinct aims.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.","answer":"It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-007","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain four effects of the Gandhian mass movements on Indian politics.","answer":"Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule. Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-048","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule.","Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-09","number":9,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 09","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["refusal to join the UN","isolation from all world affairs","automatic support for one bloc","independent judgement outside military power blocs"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["World Health Assembly","UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Dandi March"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1916","1885","1942"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["British Crown","Indian National Congress","United Nations","French government"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1919","1857","1885","1905"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["First World War","Korean War","Cold War","Second World War"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","Germany","the United States","Japan"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Mountbatten","Ripon","Dalhousie","Wavell"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe","First World War","Russian Revolution"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","a new League of Nations","a permanent single British province","continued Company rule"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Ripon","Mountbatten","Dalhousie","Curzon"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","State High Court","Rajya Sabha"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1930","1947","1942","1920"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","constitutional validity of law or executive action","membership of political parties only","profitability of companies"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","the Vernacular Press Act","the Rowlatt Act","greased cartridges"],"correct":3}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Name the principal leader associated with the INA’s later campaign and give one objective.","answer":"Subhas Chandra Bose; armed struggle to liberate India from British rule.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is the purpose of habeas corpus? What can the court require?","answer":"Protection against unlawful detention; production of the detained person and justification of detention, with release if unlawful.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the dictator associated with Fascism in Italy and the dictator associated with Nazism in Germany.","answer":"Benito Mussolini; Adolf Hitler.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain judicial review and name three kinds of government action that may raise a constitutional challenge.","answer":"Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution. A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed. Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-020","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution.","A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed.","Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Compare Early and Assertive Nationalists using aims, methods, attitude to British rule and mass participation.","answer":"Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion; Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation. Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-034","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion;","Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation.","Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-10","number":10,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 10","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","membership of political parties only","constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["First World War","formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe","Russian Revolution"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act","the Vernacular Press Act","the Salt Law"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["UNESCO","FAO","ILO","WHO"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1857","1919","1905","1885"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","continued Company rule","a new League of Nations","a permanent single British province"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1920","1942","1947","1930"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Wavell","Dalhousie","Ripon","Mountbatten"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Second World War","Cold War","Korean War","First World War"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["loss in a civil contract","unlawful detention","unpaid income tax","defeat in an election"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","five years","six years","four years"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UN Security Council","World Health Assembly","NAM secretariat","UNESCO executive office"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha","President’s electoral college","Supreme Court"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March","Chauri Chaura"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Cabinet Secretary","President","Speaker","Chief Justice"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name two Assertive Nationalist leaders.","answer":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lala Lajpat Rai: any two.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate trigger of World War I and the treaty imposed on Germany after it.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Treaty of Versailles.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers? To which House is it owed?","answer":"The ministry stands or falls together as a body and must retain the confidence of the Lok Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two major administrative consequences of the 1857 uprising.","answer":"Company rule ended and Crown rule began; office of Secretary of State for India created. Accept appropriate army reorganisation or changed princely-state policy as distinct consequences.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?","answer":"Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-077","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Judiciary / Executive","solutionSteps":["Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity.","Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.","answer":"Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-047","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances;","Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930.","Both were intended to be non-violent."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"State four clauses of the Gandhi-Irwin Pact, including at least one Congress undertaking.","answer":"Government undertook release of political prisoners not convicted of violence, withdrawal of ordinances/prosecutions, restoration of eligible confiscated property and permission for peaceful salt collection/picketing. Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and attend the Second Round Table Conference. Any four accurate clauses, with both sides represented.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-044","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Government undertook release of political prisoners not convicted of violence, withdrawal of ordinances/prosecutions, restoration of eligible confiscated property and permission for peaceful salt collection/picketing.","Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and attend the Second Round Table Conference.","Any four accurate clauses, with both sides represented."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain why the UN and NAM are not interchangeable organisations using four distinctions.","answer":"UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation; NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy. Their membership principles differ; UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ; NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-073","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation;","NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy.","Their membership principles differ;","UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ;","NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-11","number":11,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 11","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Cold War","Second World War","First World War","Korean War"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Dandi March","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1905","1919","1857","1885"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Supreme Court","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha","State High Court"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["French government","United Nations","British Crown","Indian National Congress"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Election Commission","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["ILO","UNESCO","FAO","WHO"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Ripon","Mountbatten","Wavell","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1942","1930","1922","1919"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","the United States","Germany","Japan"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1930","1947","1920","1942"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["World Health Assembly","UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1885","1916","1942","1906"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a permanent single British province","two independent dominions","a new League of Nations","continued Company rule"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["First World War","Second World War in Europe","Russian Revolution","formation of the Congress"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn in 1922? Name the incident.","answer":"Chauri Chaura violence, including burning of a police station; Gandhi judged the movement insufficiently disciplined in non-violence.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was the Doctrine of Lapse? Name the Governor-General associated with it.","answer":"Annexation of certain states when a ruler died without a recognised natural heir under the policy; Dalhousie.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name two Assertive Nationalist leaders.","answer":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lala Lajpat Rai: any two.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers? To which House is it owed?","answer":"The ministry stands or falls together as a body and must retain the confidence of the Lok Sabha.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Compare Early and Assertive Nationalists using aims, methods, attitude to British rule and mass participation.","answer":"Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion; Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation. Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-034","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion;","Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation.","Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four long-term causes of the First World War.","answer":"Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems. Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-056","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems.","Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-12","number":12,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 12","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unlawful detention","loss in a civil contract","unpaid income tax","defeat in an election"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Cabinet Secretary","Chief Justice","President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["automatic support for one bloc","refusal to join the UN","isolation from all world affairs","independent judgement outside military power blocs"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Mountbatten","Ripon","Wavell","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["British Crown","Indian National Congress","United Nations","French government"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1942","1930","1920","1947"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Mountbatten","Ripon","Curzon","Dalhousie"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","the Rowlatt Act","the Vernacular Press Act","greased cartridges"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["FAO","ILO","WHO","UNESCO"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Second World War","Cold War","Korean War","First World War"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["President’s electoral college","Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","profitability of companies","constitutional validity of law or executive action","membership of political parties only"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["the United States","Japan","Germany","Italy alone"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker","Vice-President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Chauri Chaura","Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two principal aims of the UN.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations/cooperation and promote human rights. Award two distinct aims.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was the Doctrine of Lapse? Name the Governor-General associated with it.","answer":"Annexation of certain states when a ruler died without a recognised natural heir under the policy; Dalhousie.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.","answer":"President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-076","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Parliament / Executive","solutionSteps":["President is constitutional head;","Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy;","Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.","answer":"They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-022","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access.","Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-13","number":13,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 13","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Wavell","Mountbatten","Ripon","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Second World War","First World War","Korean War","Cold War"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["FAO","WHO","ILO","UNESCO"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1905","1885","1919","1857"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Mountbatten Plan","Cabinet Mission","Dandi March","Chauri Chaura"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1916","1942","1885","1906"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker","President"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Speaker","Chief Justice","President","Cabinet Secretary"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","profitability of companies","membership of political parties only","constitutional validity of law or executive action"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","President’s electoral college"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["continued Company rule","two independent dominions","a permanent single British province","a new League of Nations"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Vernacular Press Act","the Rowlatt Act","the Salt Law","greased cartridges"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1942","1922","1919","1930"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1947","1942","1930","1920"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","five years","six years","four years"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate trigger of World War I and the treaty imposed on Germany after it.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Treaty of Versailles.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two major administrative consequences of the 1857 uprising.","answer":"Company rule ended and Crown rule began; office of Secretary of State for India created. Accept appropriate army reorganisation or changed princely-state policy as distinct consequences.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name two Assertive Nationalist leaders.","answer":"Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lala Lajpat Rai: any two.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What law did the Dandi March challenge, and in which year?","answer":"The British salt monopoly/tax law; 1930.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.","answer":"It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-007","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-14","number":14,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 14","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","World Health Assembly","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Korean War","Cold War","First World War","Second World War"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1942","1906","1916","1885"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","the United States","Japan","Germany"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a permanent single British province","a new League of Nations","continued Company rule","two independent dominions"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","Prime Minister","President","Lok Sabha Speaker"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Supreme Court"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1885","1905","1919","1857"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1920","1947","1942","1930"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Supreme Court","State High Court","Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Curzon","Mountbatten","Ripon","Dalhousie"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1919","1930","1942","1922"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["defeat in an election","unlawful detention","loss in a civil contract","unpaid income tax"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["FAO","ILO","WHO","UNESCO"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Dandi March"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe","First World War","Russian Revolution"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was appeasement? Give one pre-war example.","answer":"Concessions to an aggressor in the hope of avoiding war; Munich settlement allowing German annexation of the Sudetenland.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is the purpose of habeas corpus? What can the court require?","answer":"Protection against unlawful detention; production of the detained person and justification of detention, with release if unlawful.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers? To which House is it owed?","answer":"The ministry stands or falls together as a body and must retain the confidence of the Lok Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.","answer":"President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-076","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Parliament / Executive","solutionSteps":["President is constitutional head;","Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy;","Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"State three legislative powers of the President.","answer":"Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-010","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met.","Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain judicial review and name three kinds of government action that may raise a constitutional challenge.","answer":"Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution. A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed. Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-020","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution.","A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed.","Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare the role of armed rebellion in 1857 with the intended discipline of Gandhian mass movements. Use methods, leadership and participation.","answer":"1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives; Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation. Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-074","section":"Comparison and application","family":"1857 / Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives;","Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation.","Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.","answer":"Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-043","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline;","Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet Mission’s aim of preserving unity from the final 1947 partition arrangement using four points.","answer":"Cabinet Mission proposed a union with limited central subjects and provincial grouping; it sought to avoid division into sovereign states. The 1947 plan accepted partition and two successor dominions; transfer of power followed with different constitutional arrangements. Explain differences rather than mixing clauses from the two plans.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-054","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Cabinet Mission proposed a union with limited central subjects and provincial grouping; it sought to avoid division into sovereign states.","The 1947 plan accepted partition and two successor dominions; transfer of power followed with different constitutional arrangements.","Explain differences rather than mixing clauses from the two plans."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.","answer":"Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-061","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four distinctions between the General Assembly and Security Council.","answer":"Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-067","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-15","number":15,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 15","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","four years","six years","five years"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["membership of political parties only","constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies","merits of every parliamentary speech"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UN Security Council","UNESCO executive office","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["President","Chief Justice","Speaker","Cabinet Secretary"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["formation of the Congress","Second World War in Europe","Russian Revolution","First World War"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a permanent single British province","continued Company rule","a new League of Nations","two independent dominions"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission","Dandi March","Mountbatten Plan"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1922","1942","1919","1930"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Curzon","Ripon","Mountbatten","Dalhousie"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker","Vice-President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Korean War","First World War","Second World War","Cold War"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1942","1916","1885"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Japan","Italy alone","Germany","the United States"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["United Nations","French government","Indian National Congress","British Crown"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","State High Court"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Election Commission","Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the chief fields of work of WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: health; UNESCO: education, science and culture.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the normal term of the Lok Sabha and the term of a Rajya Sabha member.","answer":"Five years; six years respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two constitutional methods used by Early Nationalists.","answer":"Petitions/memorials; speeches, resolutions or reasoned appeals. Award two distinct methods.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two major administrative consequences of the 1857 uprising.","answer":"Company rule ended and Crown rule began; office of Secretary of State for India created. Accept appropriate army reorganisation or changed princely-state policy as distinct consequences.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.","answer":"It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-003","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.","answer":"It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-004","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution.","Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?","answer":"Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-077","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Judiciary / Executive","solutionSteps":["Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity.","Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Compare Early and Assertive Nationalists using aims, methods, attitude to British rule and mass participation.","answer":"Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion; Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation. Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-034","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion;","Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation.","Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.","answer":"Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-070","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah).","NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four distinctions between the General Assembly and Security Council.","answer":"Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-067","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-16","number":16,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 16","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Korean War","Cold War","First World War","Second World War"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Vernacular Press Act","the Salt Law","the Rowlatt Act","greased cartridges"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1942","1906","1916","1885"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a new League of Nations","continued Company rule","a permanent single British province","two independent dominions"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Election Commission","Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["defeat in an election","unlawful detention","loss in a civil contract","unpaid income tax"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["State High Court","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha","Supreme Court"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","Germany","Japan","the United States"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["isolation from all world affairs","automatic support for one bloc","independent judgement outside military power blocs","refusal to join the UN"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","membership of political parties only","constitutional validity of law or executive action","profitability of companies"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1942","1930","1947","1920"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Mountbatten","Ripon","Dalhousie","Wavell"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","President","Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","President","Speaker"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The invasion of Poland in 1939 triggered","answer":"Second World War in Europe","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-20","options":["formation of the Congress","Russian Revolution","Second World War in Europe","First World War"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["President’s electoral college","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha alone"],"correct":2}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"State two provisions of the Indian Independence Act 1947.","answer":"Creation of India and Pakistan as independent dominions; British paramountcy over princely states ended. Accept other correct distinct provisions.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What law did the Dandi March challenge, and in which year?","answer":"The British salt monopoly/tax law; 1930.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one ordinary executive appointment power and one legislative function of the President.","answer":"Appoints the Prime Minister; summons Parliament/gives assent to bills. Accept other correct ordinary powers with constitutional qualifications.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the principal leader associated with the INA’s later campaign and give one objective.","answer":"Subhas Chandra Bose; armed struggle to liberate India from British rule.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.","answer":"It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-007","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain four safeguards of judicial independence.","answer":"Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings. Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-018","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings.","Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.","answer":"Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-047","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances;","Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930.","Both were intended to be non-violent."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain four effects of the Gandhian mass movements on Indian politics.","answer":"Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule. Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-048","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule.","Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-17","number":17,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 17","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["permanent with retiring thirds","five years","four years","six years"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Dandi March","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Korean War","Cold War","First World War","Second World War"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","President’s electoral college"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha","State High Court"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act","the Vernacular Press Act"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a new League of Nations","two independent dominions","continued Company rule","a permanent single British province"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Lok Sabha Speaker","Vice-President","Prime Minister","President"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1905","1857","1885","1919"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1885","1942","1906","1916"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The UN agency chiefly concerned with world health is","answer":"WHO","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-22","options":["UNESCO","WHO","FAO","ILO"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1947","1942","1930","1920"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Dalhousie","Ripon","Wavell","Mountbatten"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Ripon","Mountbatten","Dalhousie","Curzon"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Habeas corpus is chiefly a protection against","answer":"unlawful detention","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-6","options":["unpaid income tax","unlawful detention","loss in a civil contract","defeat in an election"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two constitutional methods used by Early Nationalists.","answer":"Petitions/memorials; speeches, resolutions or reasoned appeals. Award two distinct methods.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give the year and place of the Muslim League’s founding.","answer":"1906; Dacca/Dhaka.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two principal aims of the UN.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations/cooperation and promote human rights. Award two distinct aims.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was appeasement? Give one pre-war example.","answer":"Concessions to an aggressor in the hope of avoiding war; Munich settlement allowing German annexation of the Sudetenland.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.","answer":"Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-016","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency;","Finance Commission: financial.","Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.","answer":"The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-027","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered.","Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions.","Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.","answer":"Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-031","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces.","These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.","answer":"Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-037","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division;","Lord Curzon was the Viceroy."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.","answer":"For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-040","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness.","Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.","answer":"Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-071","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation.","Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-18","number":18,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 18","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Dalhousie","Ripon","Curzon","Mountbatten"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["two independent dominions","continued Company rule","a permanent single British province","a new League of Nations"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Dandi March","Mountbatten Plan","Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court","Lok Sabha","President’s electoral college"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Treaty of Versailles was imposed principally upon","answer":"Germany","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-19","options":["Italy alone","Germany","the United States","Japan"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["Indian National Congress","United Nations","British Crown","French government"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Dalhousie","Mountbatten","Wavell","Ripon"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Vice-President","President","Lok Sabha Speaker","Prime Minister"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1857","1919","1905","1885"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","profitability of companies","constitutional validity of law or executive action","membership of political parties only"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UNESCO executive office","UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","World Health Assembly"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Vernacular Press Act","greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act","the Salt Law"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1920","1930","1942","1947"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A Money Bill can be introduced only in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-2","options":["Rajya Sabha","Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","State High Court"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["six years","five years","permanent with retiring thirds","four years"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"What was appeasement? Give one pre-war example.","answer":"Concessions to an aggressor in the hope of avoiding war; Munich settlement allowing German annexation of the Sudetenland.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn in 1922? Name the incident.","answer":"Chauri Chaura violence, including burning of a police station; Gandhi judged the movement insufficiently disciplined in non-violence.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is judicial review? Name one court that exercises it.","answer":"Examination of law/executive action for constitutional validity; Supreme Court or a High Court.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two immediate causes contributing to the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt repression/Jallianwala Bagh; Khilafat grievances. Accept two clearly different syllabus causes.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What was the Doctrine of Lapse? Name the Governor-General associated with it.","answer":"Annexation of certain states when a ruler died without a recognised natural heir under the policy; Dalhousie.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.","answer":"An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-008","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation.","Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill.","Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.","answer":"President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-076","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Parliament / Executive","solutionSteps":["President is constitutional head;","Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy;","Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.","answer":"Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-014","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions.","Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","answer":"Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-019","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.","answer":"Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-024","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Supreme Court is the national apex;","High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems.","Retirement ages are 65 and 62.","Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope.","Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.","answer":"Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-032","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear.","Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.","answer":"Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-029","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups.","Support each reason with its practical effect."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.","answer":"Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-043","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline;","Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.","answer":"Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-041","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Rowlatt Act repression;","Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy.","Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.","answer":"Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-049","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule.","Any three explained reasons."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.","answer":"Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-050","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions;","British paramountcy over princely states lapsed.","Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.","answer":"Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-057","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions.","Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four factors helping the rise of dictatorships in inter-war Europe.","answer":"Economic distress/unemployment; resentment over peace settlements or disappointed national aims; weak parliamentary governments; fear of communism and propaganda/paramilitary methods. Relate factors accurately to Germany/Italy as appropriate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-058","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Economic distress/unemployment; resentment over peace settlements or disappointed national aims; weak parliamentary governments; fear of communism and propaganda/paramilitary methods.","Relate factors accurately to Germany/Italy as appropriate."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.","answer":"It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-072","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-19","number":19,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 19","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Mountbatten","Wavell","Ripon","Dalhousie"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Supreme Court","Rajya Sabha alone","Election Commission","Lok Sabha"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["British Crown","Indian National Congress","French government","United Nations"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian National Congress was founded in","answer":"1885","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-11","options":["1919","1857","1885","1905"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March","Chauri Chaura","Cabinet Mission"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Cabinet Secretary","Chief Justice","President","Speaker"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Salt March began in","answer":"1930","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-15","options":["1922","1919","1942","1930"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act","the Vernacular Press Act","the Salt Law"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1942","1947","1920","1930"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["merits of every parliamentary speech","profitability of companies","constitutional validity of law or executive action","membership of political parties only"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Dalhousie","Curzon","Ripon","Mountbatten"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["UN Security Council","NAM secretariat","UNESCO executive office","World Health Assembly"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Indian Independence Act provided for","answer":"two independent dominions","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-17","options":["a new League of Nations","a permanent single British province","two independent dominions","continued Company rule"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1942","1906","1916","1885"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["President","Lok Sabha Speaker","Vice-President","Prime Minister"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Cold War","First World War","Korean War","Second World War"],"correct":1}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"Name the immediate military grievance of 1857 and the place where the major outbreak began on 10 May.","answer":"Greased Enfield cartridges; Meerut.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is the purpose of habeas corpus? What can the court require?","answer":"Protection against unlawful detention; production of the detained person and justification of detention, with release if unlawful.","marks":2},{"prompt":"What is collective responsibility of the Union Council of Ministers? To which House is it owed?","answer":"The ministry stands or falls together as a body and must retain the confidence of the Lok Sabha.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the 1939 invasion that triggered war in Europe and two countries that then declared war on Germany.","answer":"Invasion of Poland; Britain and France.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two major administrative consequences of the 1857 uprising.","answer":"Company rule ended and Crown rule began; office of Secretary of State for India created. Accept appropriate army reorganisation or changed princely-state policy as distinct consequences.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate trigger of World War I and the treaty imposed on Germany after it.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Treaty of Versailles.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.","answer":"Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-006","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.","answer":"The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-005","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman.","They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure.","The Speaker certifies a Money Bill.","A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.","answer":"Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-012","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission.","Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.","answer":"Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-009","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.","answer":"Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-017","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion.","Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain four safeguards of judicial independence.","answer":"Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings. Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-018","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings.","Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.","answer":"Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-030","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms.","Show how fear and distrust connected these developments."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain four changes in British policy or administration after the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-028","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.","answer":"It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-033","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-039","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness.","Link all three to constructive and oppositional work."]},{"topic":"Nationalism","prompt":"Explain four factors contributing to the formation of the Muslim League.","answer":"British encouragement of separate political identities; elite concerns about representation; the Simla Deputation/separate-electorate demand; perceived need to protect Muslim political interests in the contemporary setting. Avoid presenting the community as having one uniform view.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-038","section":"Structured theory","family":"Nationalism","solutionSteps":["British encouragement of separate political identities; elite concerns about representation; the Simla Deputation/separate-electorate demand; perceived need to protect Muslim political interests in the contemporary setting.","Avoid presenting the community as having one uniform view."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-055","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo;","Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war.","Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.","answer":"War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-075","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Wars and dictatorships / UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement.","The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three functions of the General Assembly.","answer":"Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-066","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter.","It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.","answer":"WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-069","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards;","UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation.","Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]},{"id":"histciv-paper-20","number":20,"title":"History and Civics — Paper 20","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Part I — compulsory; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Doctrine of Lapse was associated with","answer":"Dalhousie","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-9","options":["Ripon","Wavell","Dalhousie","Mountbatten"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Non-alignment implies","answer":"independent judgement outside military power blocs","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-23","options":["isolation from all world affairs","refusal to join the UN","independent judgement outside military power blocs","automatic support for one bloc"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Quit India resolution was adopted in","answer":"1942","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-16","options":["1942","1920","1930","1947"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The permanent organ primarily responsible for international peace and security is the","answer":"UN Security Council","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-21","options":["World Health Assembly","UN Security Council","UNESCO executive office","NAM secretariat"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Muslim League was founded in","answer":"1906","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-13","options":["1906","1942","1885","1916"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Judicial review examines the","answer":"constitutional validity of law or executive action","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-7","options":["constitutional validity of law or executive action","merits of every parliamentary speech","membership of political parties only","profitability of companies"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The immediate military grievance associated with the 1857 uprising concerned","answer":"greased cartridges","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-8","options":["the Salt Law","the Vernacular Press Act","greased cartridges","the Rowlatt Act"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-5","options":["Lok Sabha","President’s electoral college","Rajya Sabha alone","Supreme Court"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The assassination at Sarajevo was the immediate trigger of","answer":"First World War","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-18","options":["Second World War","Korean War","Cold War","First World War"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"A no-confidence motion against the Union government is moved in the","answer":"Lok Sabha","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-0","options":["Lok Sabha","Supreme Court","Election Commission","Rajya Sabha alone"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is","answer":"five years","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-1","options":["five years","permanent with retiring thirds","six years","four years"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Non-Cooperation Movement was withdrawn after","answer":"Chauri Chaura","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-14","options":["Cabinet Mission","Mountbatten Plan","Dandi March","Chauri Chaura"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha is the","answer":"Vice-President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-3","options":["Prime Minister","Lok Sabha Speaker","President","Vice-President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The formal head of the Union executive is the","answer":"President","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-4","options":["Chief Justice","Cabinet Secretary","Speaker","President"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The Government of India Act 1858 transferred rule to the","answer":"British Crown","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-10","options":["British Crown","French government","United Nations","Indian National Congress"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The partition of Bengal in 1905 is associated with","answer":"Curzon","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-mcq-12","options":["Ripon","Dalhousie","Mountbatten","Curzon"],"correct":3}]},{"label":"2","prompt":"Answer all subparts.","marks":14,"parts":[{"prompt":"State one objective each of Swadeshi and Boycott.","answer":"Swadeshi promoted indigenous production/use; Boycott rejected foreign goods/institutions as a means of pressure and self-reliance.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Give two constitutional methods used by Early Nationalists.","answer":"Petitions/memorials; speeches, resolutions or reasoned appeals. Award two distinct methods.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Distinguish the Council of Ministers from the Cabinet.","answer":"The Council includes all categories of ministers; the Cabinet is the smaller senior decision-making body within it.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Who presides over the Lok Sabha? Who is the ex-officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha?","answer":"Speaker; Vice-President respectively.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State the year of Quit India and the central demand of the movement.","answer":"1942; end British rule/withdraw from India.","marks":2},{"prompt":"Name the immediate trigger of World War I and the treaty imposed on Germany after it.","answer":"Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Treaty of Versailles.","marks":2},{"prompt":"State two principal aims of the UN.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations/cooperation and promote human rights. Award two distinct aims.","marks":2}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Part II A — Civics; 20 marks","questions":[{"label":"3","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.","answer":"The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-002","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.","answer":"The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-001","section":"Structured theory","family":"Parliament","solutionSteps":["The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return.","The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations."]},{"topic":"Parliament","prompt":"Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.","answer":"President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-076","section":"Comparison and application","family":"Parliament / Executive","solutionSteps":["President is constitutional head;","Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy;","Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence."]}]},{"label":"4","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.","answer":"Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-013","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.","answer":"Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-015","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision."]},{"topic":"Executive","prompt":"Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.","answer":"A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-011","section":"Structured theory","family":"Executive","solutionSteps":["A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President.","The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.","answer":"The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-023","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.","answer":"Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-021","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty.","Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform."]},{"topic":"Judiciary","prompt":"Explain four safeguards of judicial independence.","answer":"Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings. Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-018","section":"Structured theory","family":"Judiciary","solutionSteps":["Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings.","Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard."]}]}],"attempt":2,"marks":20,"instructions":"Answer any 2 questions."},{"title":"Part II B — History; 30 marks","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.","answer":"Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-025","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts.","Link each policy to the affected group."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.","answer":"Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-026","section":"Structured theory","family":"1857","solutionSteps":["Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself."]},{"topic":"1857","prompt":"Compare the role of armed rebellion in 1857 with the intended discipline of Gandhian mass movements. Use methods, leadership and participation.","answer":"1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives; Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation. Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-074","section":"Comparison and application","family":"1857 / Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives;","Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation.","Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.","answer":"Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-045","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.","answer":"Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-043","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline;","Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle."]},{"topic":"Gandhian movements","prompt":"Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.","answer":"Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-046","section":"Structured theory","family":"Gandhian movements","solutionSteps":["Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise.","Explain their combined effect."]}]},{"label":"8","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.","answer":"They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-053","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.","answer":"Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-051","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements.","State two specific accurate terms."]},{"topic":"Independence and Partition","prompt":"Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.","answer":"Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-052","section":"Structured theory","family":"Independence and Partition","solutionSteps":["Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response.","Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily."]}]},{"label":"9","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.","answer":"League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-063","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["League followed WWI;","UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements.","Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.","answer":"Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-059","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms.","Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism."]},{"topic":"Wars and dictatorships","prompt":"Explain four consequences of the Second World War.","answer":"Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-062","section":"Structured theory","family":"Wars and dictatorships","solutionSteps":["Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath.","Use four distinct consequences."]}]},{"label":"10","prompt":"","marks":10,"parts":[{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State three purposes of the United Nations.","answer":"Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-064","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights.","Explain each distinct purpose."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.","answer":"Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-068","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested.","It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences."]},{"topic":"UN and NAM","prompt":"Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.","answer":"Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"histciv-071","section":"Structured theory","family":"UN and NAM","solutionSteps":["Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation.","Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict."]}]}],"attempt":3,"marks":30,"instructions":"Answer any 3 questions."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":0,"adaptation":"","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper."]}],"plan":{"strategy":"Use choice to reduce deep preparation: two Civics units plus a backup; three History clusters plus a backup. Keep Gandhi and the broader 1905–1947 sequence to a concise compulsory review unless your backup answers are weaker.","core":["Civics: Parliament and Union Executive. Prepare membership/terms, procedures, powers, accountability and scenario-based questions.","History cluster 1 — 1857: political, economic, socio-religious and military causes; immediate trigger; failure; Crown rule and subsequent changes.","History cluster 2 — World Wars, Nazism and Fascism: causes, immediate triggers, Versailles, dictatorships, consequences and international order. This is a cluster, not a tiny single chapter.","History cluster 3 — UN, its relevant organs/agencies, and NAM: composition/functions, limits, aims and non-alignment versus neutrality/isolation."],"backup":["Civics: Judiciary, especially Supreme Court/High Court jurisdiction, writs, independence and judicial review.","History: Early and Assertive Nationalism. If a full backup question cannot be answered from that preparation, add one clearly defined later movement rather than assuming 1857/wars/UN must fill three slots."],"light":["Gandhian movements: brief cause–date–method–result tables for Non-Cooperation, Civil Disobedience and Quit India. Do not spend equal long-answer time here when the core route works.","1905–1947: retain partition of Bengal, Muslim League, Lucknow Pact, INA, Cabinet Mission/Mountbatten and Independence Act essentials for compulsory MCQs/shorts. This is a skim route, not a claim that the period has no exam relevance."],"rationale":"Recent papers offer recognisable 1857, international-conflict and UN/NAM clusters, making a selective long-answer route practical. The choice is any three of five History questions and any two of three Civics questions. No regulation guarantees the same chapter grouping every year. The 2026 paper also contained Gandhi and Independence/Partition choices; those can be left as unchosen long answers when three other answers are secure.","source":"https://cisce.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/ICSE-2027-SPECIMEN-501-HISTORY-CIVICS-QP-MS.pdf","sessions":[["Parliament","75 min","Make matched Lok Sabha/Rajya Sabha comparisons. Write two 10-mark answers on law-making, Money Bills and executive control. Mark points separately; do not repeat one power in three forms."],["Executive","75 min","Map President, Vice-President, Prime Minister and Council of Ministers. Practise appointment, functions, ordinary constitutional operation and collective responsibility; finish two timed choices."],["1857","65 min","Sort causes into four headings. Learn one explained example for each, then distinguish immediate cause, failure and consequences. Attempt three 3+3+4 sets across these headings."],["Wars and dictatorships","90 min","Make separate WWI/WWII cause-and-result tables and Nazism/Fascism features. Practise two complete 10-mark questions; never interchange the two immediate triggers or treaties."],["UN and NAM","75 min","Prepare organ/agency functions and examples; learn permanent-member/veto limits and NAM principles. Attempt two 10-mark questions and 15 compulsory items."],["Backups","90 min","Prepare Judiciary and Early/Assertive Nationalism sufficiently to write one full choice from each. A backup is usable only after two timed 7/10 answers."],["Light chronology sweep","45 min","Make a one-page 1905–1947 table. Answer 25 MCQs and ten two-mark questions, including Gandhi. Correct names, dates and cause/result confusions; stop before turning this into full essay revision."],["Timed selection","2 hours + 30 min review","Attempt Part I, two Civics and three History questions. Select after reading every subpart. If any core cluster repeatedly falls below 7/10, prepare another backup before dropping further material."]]},"edition":"2026-11-16-worked"};
